        order: 6,
        title: 'Responsible AI, governance, and security',
        body: `Responsible AI turns principles such as fairness, transparency, privacy, safety, robustness, and accountability into decisions across the lifecycle. Governance starts with an inventory of systems, named owners, intended use, affected people, risk classification, approval criteria, documentation, and routes for challenge or appeal. Impact depends on context: a writing assistant and a clinical prioritization model require different evidence and oversight. Teams should examine data provenance, consent or legal basis, representation, accessibility, explainability needs, and whether automation changes power or opportunity. AI security includes familiar risks such as identity abuse and data leakage plus model-specific threats such as poisoning, extraction, evasion, prompt injection, insecure tool use, and unsafe model supply chains. Controls include access boundaries, data minimization, isolated retrieval, content handling, red teaming, output validation, rate limits, monitoring, and incident response. Human oversight must be meaningful: reviewers need time, authority, competence, and enough information to disagree. Accountability remains with the organization even when a vendor supplies the model.`,
