        body: `Machine Learning Operations and Large Language Model Operations apply lifecycle discipline to data, models, prompts, retrieval, tools, and evaluations. Teams version code, datasets, features, model artifacts, instructions, policies, and test suites so a production result can be reproduced and investigated. A controlled pipeline moves changes through validation, security checks, approval, staged release, and rollback. Registries record ownership, lineage, intended use, limitations, and deployment status. Monitoring should connect technical signals to user and business outcomes: input drift, output quality, refusal patterns, unsafe content, latency, cost, tool errors, bias, and human override. Logs need privacy controls because prompts and retrieved context may contain sensitive information. Incident plans should define detection, triage, containment, provider coordination, rollback or model substitution, stakeholder communication, and learning. Teams must also watch model and API deprecations, dependency vulnerabilities, expiring data rights, and silent provider changes. Retirement removes endpoints, credentials, replicas, stored prompts, and downstream dependencies while preserving required evidence.`,
